<!-- ==== ABSTRACT ==== -->
<section class="section" id="abstract">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <div class="section-heading reveal">
      <span class="eyebrow">Abstract</span>
      <h2>Co-adapting continuous semantics and discrete generation</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="abstract-block reveal">
      <p>
        Hierarchical language generation separates continuous semantic modeling from discrete text
        realization, but its effectiveness depends on what information the latent space should
        compress and how the discrete generator should use it. We propose <b>Cola2</b>, which jointly
        studies continuous semantic compression, the discrete reconstruction–generation balance, and
        their co-adaptation. Cola2 dynamically segments text into semantic spans, controls the
        compression rate with an auxiliary-loss-free negative-feedback mechanism, and combines
        reconstruction and generation supervision to learn compact and predictive latent
        representations.
      </p>
      <p>
        A block-causal masked diffusion model (MDM) decoder is jointly trained under clean, noisy,
        and dropped latent conditions, and classifier-free guidance (CFG) adjusts the balance between
        semantic reconstruction and autonomous generation. During training, token-prediction gradients
        update the encoder through the latents, driving the continuous representation and discrete
        generation to co-adapt, while Flow Matching prior learning is decoupled from representation
        learning via stop-gradient. At inference, the generated text is re-encoded under the fixed
        span layout, keeping the latent history consistent with the actual text context.
      </p>
      <p>
        Systematic component analyses and controlled comparisons on five downstream tasks with about
        <b>210B training tokens</b> validate the core designs and show competitive, unsaturated
        scaling. With only <b>346.03B source tokens</b>, the unconverged checkpoint reaches a
        <b>60.10 eight-task average</b> and surpasses Llama-3-8B on HellaSwag and StoryCloze despite
        its over <b>43× larger</b> reported pretraining budget.
      </p>
      <p>
        These results indicate effective transfer of the Qwen-initialized causal decoder's generation
        capability and substantial scaling headroom for hierarchical language generation. More
        broadly, continuous-latent hierarchical modeling can combine effectively with established
        token-space generation paradigms, including autoregressive and masked-diffusion decoders.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ==== METHOD ==== -->
<section class="section is-soft" id="method">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-heading reveal">
      <span class="eyebrow">Method</span>
      <h2>The Cola2 workflow</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        A compressed continuous latent prior organizes semantics; a capable token-space decoder
        reconstructs those semantics and generates the remaining detail.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="cola-figure reveal">
      <img
        src="static/images/cola2-workflow.svg"
        alt="Cola2 method overview: compression VAE, balanced discrete decoder, detached latent DiT training, and block-wise inference."
        loading="lazy"
        style="width:min(100%, 1000px);">
      <figcaption>
        <b>Method overview from the paper.</b> Stage 1 jointly trains the compression encoder and
        reconstruction–generation balanced decoder. Stage 2 additionally trains a DiT on detached
        latents. At inference, the DiT generates latents block by block and the decoder realizes them
        as tokens.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="method-tldr">
      <div class="stage reveal reveal-delay-1">
        <span class="stage-tag">Stage 1</span>
        <h4>Semantic compression</h4>
        <p>Dynamic segmentation assigns latent positions to content-aware spans. A gradient-free controller keeps the mean compression rate near its target without competing with representation learning.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stage reveal reveal-delay-2">
        <span class="stage-tag">Stage 2</span>
        <h4>Detached latent prior</h4>
        <p>A block-causal DiT fits the online latent distribution with Flow Matching. Its gradient is stopped at the encoder, while token-side gradients continue to shape the representation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stage reveal reveal-delay-3">
        <span class="stage-tag">Inference</span>
        <h4>Propose → decode → re-encode</h4>
        <p>The DiT proposes a latent block, the decoder realizes tokens, and fixed-span feedback re-encodes those tokens so latent and discrete histories remain aligned.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ==== EXPERIMENTS ==== -->
<section class="section" id="experiments">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-heading reveal">
      <span class="eyebrow">Experiments</span>
      <h2>Three questions across the hierarchy</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        We study how to construct a compressed semantic latent space, how to train its continuous
        generator, and how the discrete decoder should balance reconstruction with generation.
      </p>
    </div>

    <details class="detail-block reveal" open>
      <summary>1 · How do we build a compressed, predictive semantic latent space?</summary>
      <div class="detail-body">
        <div class="takeaway">
          <b>Main finding.</b> Dynamic semantic segmentation and generation-oriented supervision are
          both essential. At 2× compression, the best dynamic configuration reaches 29.4% on LAMBADA
          and slightly surpasses the uncompressed model; co-evolving the encoder and decoder with
          generative supervision reaches 58.2%.
        </div>
        <div class="figure-grid cols-2">
          <figure class="cola-figure">
            <img src="static/images/figures/stage1_latent/fig1_segmentation_and_rate_control.svg" alt="Dynamic segmentation and rate-control comparison." loading="lazy">
            <figcaption><b>Segmentation and rate control.</b> Content-aware spans beat fixed and random grouping; auxiliary-free feedback beats DLCM.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="cola-figure">
            <img src="static/images/figures/stage1_latent/fig2_vae_architecture.svg" alt="VAE encoder architecture and pooling ablations." loading="lazy">
            <figcaption><b>VAE architecture.</b> Layer allocation matters more than depth; intra-span bidirectional attention is best.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="cola-figure">
            <img src="static/images/figures/stage1_latent/fig3_latent_width_and_max_span.svg" alt="Latent width and maximum span-length ablations." loading="lazy">
            <figcaption><b>Capacity.</b> Latent width has an interior optimum and maximum span length is non-monotonic.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="cola-figure">
            <img src="static/images/figures/stage1_latent/fig4b_span_length_distribution.svg" alt="Span-length distributions." loading="lazy">
            <figcaption><b>Span distribution.</b> Larger caps reduce forcibly truncated semantic spans.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="cola-figure">
            <img src="static/images/figures/stage1_latent/fig5_generative_loss_on_encoder.svg" alt="Generative supervision ablations." loading="lazy">
            <figcaption><b>Generative supervision.</b> Predictive objectives and encoder–decoder co-evolution are complementary.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="detail-block reveal">
      <summary>2 · How should the continuous latent generator be trained?</summary>
      <div class="detail-body">
        <div class="takeaway">
          <b>Main finding.</b> Keep adapting the VAE with token-side supervision, but detach the
          Flow Matching gradient from the encoder. A moderate latent block size is best, the noise
          schedule must match the representation, and inference saturates at roughly eight DiT steps.
        </div>
        <div class="figure-grid cols-2">
          <figure class="cola-figure">
            <img src="static/images/figures/stage2_dit/fig6_vae_policy_and_noise_schedule.svg" alt="VAE update policy and DiT noise-schedule results." loading="lazy">
            <figcaption><b>Update policy and calibration.</b> Trainable VAE plus detached DiT gradient performs best; the schedule must match the latent.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="cola-figure">
            <img src="static/images/figures/stage2_dit/fig8_block_size.svg" alt="DiT block size and model-capacity results." loading="lazy">
            <figcaption><b>Block size.</b> Moderate blocks are optimal; larger DiTs tolerate broader blocks.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="cola-figure">
            <img src="static/images/figures/stage2_dit/fig9_dit_infer_steps.svg" alt="Latent generator CFG and inference-step sweeps." loading="lazy">
            <figcaption><b>Inference.</b> Strong CFG helps and DiT denoising saturates at about eight steps.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="detail-block reveal">
      <summary>3 · How should the decoder balance reconstruction and generation?</summary>
      <div class="detail-body">
        <div class="takeaway">
          <b>Main finding.</b> The decoder should train mostly under noisy and dropped latent
          conditions while retaining clean reconstruction examples. The selected 0.4 / 0.4 / 0.2
          drop–noise–clean mixture reaches 44.7%, and shuffled latents cause the largest degradation,
          confirming that the decoder uses aligned semantic content.
        </div>
        <div class="figure-grid cols-2">
          <figure class="cola-figure">
            <img src="static/images/figures/stage3_decoder/fig10_decoder_data_partition.svg" alt="Decoder clean, noisy, and dropped condition mixture." loading="lazy">
            <figcaption><b>Data partition.</b> A 0.4 / 0.4 / 0.2 drop–noise–clean mixture gives the best balance.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="cola-figure">
            <img src="static/images/figures/stage3_decoder/fig11_decoder_cfg.svg" alt="Decoder CFG and parameter-allocation results." loading="lazy">
            <figcaption><b>Guidance and allocation.</b> CFG must match training; a 2B DiT with a 1B decoder is the best 3B split.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="cola-figure">
            <img src="static/images/figures/stage3_decoder/fig12_decoder_size.svg" alt="Decoder size scaling results." loading="lazy">
            <figcaption><b>Decoder size.</b> Accuracy improves monotonically with decoder capacity under a fixed DiT.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="cola-figure">
            <img src="static/images/figures/stage3_decoder/fig13_demask_and_latent_ablation.svg" alt="Demasking steps and latent ablation." loading="lazy">
            <figcaption><b>Latent use.</b> Eight-by-eight steps are optimal; shuffled latents cause the largest degradation.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="detail-block reveal">
      <summary>4 · Do the gains continue as training scales?</summary>
      <div class="detail-body">
        <div class="takeaway">
          <b>Main finding.</b> Under controlled training to approximately 210B tokens, Cola-MDM
          achieves the strongest overall scaling. Compressed Cola2-MDM converges more slowly, but
          continues closing its gap to LLaDA after about 150B tokens without saturation.
        </div>
        <div class="figure-grid cols-2">
          <figure class="cola-figure">
            <img src="static/images/figures/scaling/scaling_scatter_trend.svg" alt="Controlled scaling trends for AR, LLaDA, Cola DLM, Cola-MDM, and Cola2-MDM." loading="lazy">
            <figcaption><b>Controlled scaling.</b> Compression retains strong semantic-task scaling while the latent DiT continues to improve.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="cola-figure">
            <img src="static/images/figures/scaling/best_decoder_cfg_trend.svg" alt="Optimal decoder CFG weight over the controlled scaling run." loading="lazy">
            <figcaption><b>Decoder CFG trend.</b> The optimum remains near −0.9 and drifts gradually toward zero during training.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

