<!-- ==== EXPERIMENTAL SUMMARY ==== -->
<section class="section" id="summary">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <div class="section-heading reveal">
      <span class="eyebrow">Experiments</span>
      <h2>Summary</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="abstract-block reveal">
      <p>The results support the design of <b>Cola2</b> in three respects.</p>
      <p>
        <b>Semantic compression:</b> dynamic segmentation and generative supervision keep the
        latents strongly semantic at high compression rates; the former slightly surpasses the
        uncompressed model with an appropriate <span class="eq">L<sub>max</sub></span>, and the
        latter raises accuracy from <b>15.7%</b> to <b>26.7%</b>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Reconstruction–generation balance:</b> mixed training dominated by noisy and dropped
        conditions enables the decoder both to faithfully use the latent (shuffling the latent costs
        <b>12.2 points</b>) and to autonomously generate the residual information.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Continuous–discrete co-adaptation:</b> the information placed in the latent should be
        determined by the decoder; co-training the encoder and decoder yields a <b>5.9-point gain</b>,
        and both continue to benefit from more training data, giving the hierarchical model scaling
        trends comparable to or better than AR and LLaDA.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ==== LIMITATIONS & FUTURE PROSPECTS ==== -->
<section class="section is-soft">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <div class="section-heading reveal">
      <span class="eyebrow">Outlook</span>
      <h2>Limitations &amp; Future Prospects</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="abstract-block reveal">
      <p>
        Although our results provide initial evidence for the feasibility, competitiveness, and
        scaling potential of <b>Cola2</b>, we view the current system as a starting point rather than
        a finished endpoint.
      </p>
      <p>
        First, the model remains far from convergence, especially the latent DiT, while the present
        training scale is limited by available resources. Larger models, longer training, and broader
        data and compute budgets are therefore needed to establish its performance ceiling.
      </p>
      <p>
        Second, compression reduces the dominant linear and attention FLOPs of the latent prior in
        theory, but end-to-end generation speed also depends on the decoder, CFG branches, latent
        feedback, and hardware utilization. Infrastructure co-design—including specialized kernels,
        cache management, and parallel scheduling—is needed to translate theoretical savings into
        practical speedups.
      </p>
      <p>
        Third, <b>Cola2</b> already shows compatibility with autoregressive and masked-diffusion
        decoders, but their co-training objectives, inference trade-offs, and optimal division of
        labor remain underexplored.
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, the continuous latent space may provide a shared interface between text and other
        continuous modalities such as vision, opening a path toward unified latent representations,
        cross-modal priors, and unified understanding and generation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

